          "source_text": "Choose filtrations <MATH_0001> on <MATH_0002> and <MATH_0003>. Then consider the filtration\non <MATH_0004> given by\n<MATH_0005>\nThis is clearly a differential graded <MATH_0006>-submodule. We see that\n<MATH_0007>\nfor example because the filtration of <MATH_0008> is split in the category\nof graded <MATH_0009>-modules. Since by assumption the quotients on the right\nhand side are isomorphic to direct sums of shifts of <MATH_0010> and\n<MATH_0011> and since\n<MATH_0012>,\nwe conclude that the left hand side is a direct sum of shifts\nof <MATH_0013> as a differential graded <MATH_0014>-module.\n(Warning: <MATH_0015> does not have a structure of <MATH_0016>-bimodule.)\nThis proves the first statement of the lemma.\nThe second statement is immediate from\nthe definition of the functor in\nDifferential Graded Algebra, Lemma <REF_0001>.\n",
